   95: /*
   96:  MISSION STATEMENT
   97:  =================
   98:  - Easy to use to create code-driven and data-driven tools.
   99:  - Easy to use to create ad hoc short-lived tools and long-lived, more elaborate tools.
  100:  - Easy to hack and improve.
  101:  - Minimize setup and maintenance.
  102:  - Minimize state storage on user side.
  103:  - Minimize state synchronization.
  104:  - Portable, minimize dependencies, run on target (consoles, phones, etc.).
  105:  - Efficient runtime and memory consumption.
  106:  Designed primarily for developers and content-creators, not the typical end-user!
  107:  Some of the current weaknesses (which we aim to address in the future) includes:
  108:  - Doesn't look fancy by default.
  109:  - Limited layout features, intricate layouts are typically crafted in code.
  110:  CONTROLS GUIDE
  111:  ==============
  112:  - MOUSE CONTROLS
  113:    - Mouse wheel:                   Scroll vertically.
  114:    - Shift+Mouse wheel:             Scroll horizontally.
  115:    - Click [X]:                     Close a window, available when 'bool* p_open' is passed to ImGui::Begin().
  116:    - Click ^, Double-Click title:   Collapse window.
  117:    - Drag on corner/border:         Resize window (double-click to auto fit window to its contents).
  118:    - Drag on any empty space:       Move window (unless io.ConfigWindowsMoveFromTitleBarOnly = true).
  119:    - Left-click outside popup:      Close popup stack (right-click over underlying popup: Partially close popup stack).
  120:  - TEXT EDITOR
  121:    - Hold Shift or Drag Mouse:      Select text.
  122:    - Ctrl+Left/Right:               Word jump.
  123:    - Ctrl+Shift+Left/Right:         Select words.
  124:    - Ctrl+A or Double-Click:        Select All.
  125:    - Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V:        Use OS clipboard.
  126:    - Ctrl+Z                         Undo.
  127:    - Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z:        Redo.
  128:    - ESCAPE:                        Revert text to its original value.
  129:    - On macOS, controls are automatically adjusted to match standard macOS text editing and behaviors.
  130:      (for 99% of shortcuts, Ctrl is replaced by Cmd on macOS).
  131:  - KEYBOARD CONTROLS
  132:    - Basic:
  133:      - Tab, Shift+Tab               Cycle through text editable fields.
  134:      - Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab     Cycle through windows.
  135:      - Ctrl+Click                   Input text into a Slider or Drag widget.
  136:    - Extended features with `io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard`:
  137:      - Tab, Shift+Tab:              Cycle through every items.
  138:      - Arrow keys                   Move through items using directional navigation. Tweak value.
  139:      - Arrow keys + Alt, Shift      Tweak slower, tweak faster (when using arrow keys).
  140:      - Enter                        Activate item (prefer text input when possible).
  141:      - Space                        Activate item (prefer tweaking with arrows when possible).
  142:      - Escape                       Deactivate item, leave child window, close popup.
  143:      - Page Up, Page Down           Previous page, next page.
  144:      - Home, End                    Scroll to top, scroll to bottom.
  145:      - Alt                          Toggle between scrolling layer and menu layer.
  146:      - Ctrl+Tab then Ctrl+Arrows    Move window. Hold Shift to resize instead of moving.
  147:    - Output when ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard set,
  148:      - io.WantCaptureKeyboard flag is set when keyboard is claimed.
  149:      - io.NavActive: true when a window is focused and it doesn't have the ImGuiWindowFlags_NoNavInputs flag set.
  150:      - io.NavVisible: true when the navigation cursor is visible (usually goes to back false when mouse is used).
  151:  - GAMEPAD CONTROLS
  152:    - Enable with 'io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableGamepad'.
  153:    - Particularly useful to use Dear ImGui on a console system (e.g. PlayStation, Switch, Xbox) without a mouse!
  154:    - Download controller mapping PNG/PSD at http://dearimgui.com/controls_sheets
  155:    - Backend support: backend needs to:
  156:       - Set 'io.BackendFlags |= ImGuiBackendFlags_HasGamepad' + call io.AddKeyEvent/AddKeyAnalogEvent() with ImGuiKey_Gamepad_XXX keys.
  157:       - For analog values (0.0f to 1.0f), backend is responsible to handling a dead-zone and rescaling inputs accordingly.
  158:         Backend code will probably need to transform your raw inputs (such as e.g. remapping your 0.2..0.9 raw input range to 0.0..1.0 imgui range, etc.).
  159:    - If you need to share inputs between your game and the Dear ImGui interface, the easiest approach is to go all-or-nothing,
  160:      with a buttons combo to toggle the target. Please reach out if you think the game vs navigation input sharing could be improved.
  161:  - REMOTE INPUTS SHARING & MOUSE EMULATION
  162:    - PS4/PS5 users: Consider emulating a mouse cursor with DualShock touch pad or a spare analog stick as a mouse-emulation fallback.
  163:    - Consoles/Tablet/Phone users: Consider using a Synergy 1.x server (on your PC) + run examples/libs/synergy/uSynergy.c (on your console/tablet/phone app)
  164:      in order to share your PC mouse/keyboard.
  165:    - See https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki/Useful-Extensions#remoting for other remoting solutions.
  166:    - On a TV/console system where readability may be lower or mouse inputs may be awkward, you may want to set the io.ConfigNavMoveSetMousePos flag.
  167:      Enabling io.ConfigNavMoveSetMousePos + ImGuiBackendFlags_HasSetMousePos instructs Dear ImGui to move your mouse cursor along with navigation movements.
  168:      When enabled, the NewFrame() function may alter 'io.MousePos' and set 'io.WantSetMousePos' to notify you that it wants the mouse cursor to be moved.
  169:      When that happens your backend NEEDS to move the OS or underlying mouse cursor on the next frame. Some of the backends in examples/ do that.
  170:      (If you set the NavEnableSetMousePos flag but don't honor 'io.WantSetMousePos' properly, Dear ImGui will misbehave as it will see your mouse moving back & forth!)
  171:      (In a setup when you may not have easy control over the mouse cursor, e.g. uSynergy.c doesn't expose moving remote mouse cursor, you may want
  172:      to set a boolean to ignore your other external mouse positions until the external source is moved again.)
  173:  PROGRAMMER GUIDE
  174:  ================
  175:  READ FIRST
  176:  ----------
  177:  - Remember to check the wonderful Wiki: https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki
  178:  - Your code creates the UI every frame of your application loop, if your code doesn't run the UI is gone!
  179:    The UI can be highly dynamic, there are no construction or destruction steps, less superfluous
  180:    data retention on your side, less state duplication, less state synchronization, fewer bugs.
  181:  - Call and read ImGui::ShowDemoWindow() for demo code demonstrating most features.
  182:    Or browse https://pthom.github.io/imgui_manual_online/manual/imgui_manual.html for interactive web version.
  183:  - The library is designed to be built from sources. Avoid pre-compiled binaries and packaged versions. See imconfig.h to configure your build.
  184:  - Dear ImGui is an implementation of the IMGUI paradigm (immediate-mode graphical user interface, a term coined by Casey Muratori).
  185:    You can learn about IMGUI principles at http://www.johno.se/book/imgui.html, http://mollyrocket.com/861 & more links in Wiki.
  186:  - Dear ImGui is a "single pass" rasterizing implementation of the IMGUI paradigm, aimed at ease of use and high-performances.
  187:    For every application frame, your UI code will be called only once. This is in contrast to e.g. Unity's implementation of an IMGUI,
  188:    where the UI code is called multiple times ("multiple passes") from a single entry point. There are pros and cons to both approaches.
  189:  - Our origin is on the top-left. In axis aligned bounding boxes, Min = top-left, Max = bottom-right.
  190:  - Please make sure you have asserts enabled (IM_ASSERT redirects to assert() by default, but can be redirected).
  191:    If you get an assert, read the messages and comments around the assert.
  192:  - This codebase aims to be highly optimized:
  193:    - A typical idle frame should never call malloc/free.
  194:    - We rely on a maximum of constant-time or O(N) algorithms. Limiting searches/scans as much as possible.
  195:    - We put particular energy in making sure performances are decent with typical "Debug" build settings as well.
  196:      Which mean we tend to avoid over-relying on "zero-cost abstraction" as they aren't zero-cost at all.
  197:  - This codebase aims to be both highly opinionated and highly flexible:
  198:    - This code works because of the things it choose to solve or not solve.
  199:    - C++: this is a pragmatic C-ish codebase: we don't use fancy C++ features, we don't include C++ headers,
  200:      and ImGui:: is a namespace. We rarely use member functions (and when we did, I am mostly regretting it now).
  201:      This is to increase compatibility, increase maintainability and facilitate use from other languages.
  202:    - C++: ImVec2/ImVec4 do not expose math operators by default, because it is expected that you use your own math types.
  203:      See FAQ "How can I use my own math types instead of ImVec2/ImVec4?" for details about setting up imconfig.h for that.
  204:      We can can optionally export math operators for ImVec2/ImVec4 using IMGUI_DEFINE_MATH_OPERATORS, which we use internally.
  205:    - C++: pay attention that ImVector<> manipulates plain-old-data and does not honor construction/destruction
  206:      (so don't use ImVector in your code or at our own risk!).
  207:    - Building: We don't use nor mandate a build system for the main library.
  208:      This is in an effort to ensure that it works in the real world aka with any esoteric build setup.
  209:      This is also because providing a build system for the main library would be of little-value.
  210:      The build problems are almost never coming from the main library but from specific backends.
  211:  HOW TO UPDATE TO A NEWER VERSION OF DEAR IMGUI
  212:  ----------------------------------------------
  213:  - Update submodule or copy/overwrite every file.
  214:  - About imconfig.h:
  215:    - You may modify your copy of imconfig.h, in this case don't overwrite it.
  216:    - or you may locally branch to modify imconfig.h and merge/rebase latest.
  217:    - or you may '#define IMGUI_USER_CONFIG "my_config_file.h"' globally from your build system to
  218:      specify a custom path for your imconfig.h file and instead not have to modify the default one.
  219:  - Overwrite all the sources files except for imconfig.h (if you have modified your copy of imconfig.h)
  220:  - Or maintain your own branch where you have imconfig.h modified as a top-most commit which you can regularly rebase over "master".
  221:  - You can also use '#define IMGUI_USER_CONFIG "my_config_file.h" to redirect configuration to your own file.
  222:  - Read the "API BREAKING CHANGES" section (below). This is where we list occasional API breaking changes.
  223:    If a function/type has been renamed / or marked obsolete, try to fix the name in your code before it is permanently removed
  224:    from the public API. If you have a problem with a missing function/symbols, search for its name in the code, there will
  225:    likely be a comment about it. Please report any issue to the GitHub page!
  226:  - To find out usage of old API, you can add '#define IMGUI_DISABLE_OBSOLETE_FUNCTIONS' in your configuration file.
  227:  - Try to keep your copy of Dear ImGui reasonably up to date!
  228:  GETTING STARTED WITH INTEGRATING DEAR IMGUI IN YOUR CODE/ENGINE
  229:  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  230:  - See https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/wiki/Getting-Started.
  231:  - Run and study the examples and demo in imgui_demo.cpp to get acquainted with the library.
  232:  - In the majority of cases you should be able to use unmodified backends files available in the backends/ folder.
  233:  - Add the Dear ImGui source files + selected backend source files to your projects or using your preferred build system.
  234:    It is recommended you build and statically link the .cpp files as part of your project and NOT as a shared library (DLL).
  235:  - You can later customize the imconfig.h file to tweak some compile-time behavior, such as integrating Dear ImGui types with your own maths types.
  236:  - When using Dear ImGui, your programming IDE is your friend: follow the declaration of variables, functions and types to find comments about them.
  237:  - Dear ImGui never touches or knows about your GPU state. The only function that knows about GPU is the draw function that you provide.
  238:    Effectively it means you can create widgets at any time in your code, regardless of considerations of being in "update" vs "render"
  239:    phases of your own application. All rendering information is stored into command-lists that you will retrieve after calling ImGui::Render().
  240:  - Refer to the backends and demo applications in the examples/ folder for instruction on how to setup your code.
  241:  - If you are running over a standard OS with a common graphics API, you should be able to use unmodified imgui_impl_*** files from the examples/ folder.
  242:  HOW A SIMPLE APPLICATION MAY LOOK LIKE
  243:  --------------------------------------
  244:  USING THE EXAMPLE BACKENDS (= imgui_impl_XXX.cpp files from the backends/ folder).
  245:  The sub-folders in examples/ contain examples applications following this structure.
  246:      // Application init: create a dear imgui context, setup some options, load fonts
  247:      ImGui::CreateContext();
  248:      ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();
  249:      // TODO: Set optional io.ConfigFlags values, e.g. 'io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard' to enable keyboard controls.
  250:      // TODO: Fill optional fields of the io structure later.
  251:      // TODO: Load TTF/OTF fonts if you don't want to use the default font.
  252:      // Initialize helper Platform and Renderer backends (here we are using imgui_impl_win32.cpp and imgui_impl_dx11.cpp)
  253:      ImGui_ImplWin32_Init(hwnd);
  254:      ImGui_ImplDX11_Init(g_pd3dDevice, g_pd3dDeviceContext);
  255:      // Application main loop
  256:      while (true)
  257:      {
  258:          // Feed inputs to dear imgui, start new frame
  259:          ImGui_ImplDX11_NewFrame();
  260:          ImGui_ImplWin32_NewFrame();
  261:          ImGui::NewFrame();
  262:          // Any application code here
  263:          ImGui::Text("Hello, world!");
  264:          // Render dear imgui into framebuffer
  265:          ImGui::Render();
  266:          ImGui_ImplDX11_RenderDrawData(ImGui::GetDrawData());
  267:          g_pSwapChain->Present(1, 0);
  268:      }
  269:      // Shutdown
  270:      ImGui_ImplDX11_Shutdown();
  271:      ImGui_ImplWin32_Shutdown();
  272:      ImGui::DestroyContext();
  273:  To decide whether to dispatch mouse/keyboard inputs to Dear ImGui to the rest of your application,
  274:  you should read the 'io.WantCaptureMouse', 'io.WantCaptureKeyboard' and 'io.WantTextInput' flags!
  275:  Please read the FAQ entry "How can I tell whether to dispatch mouse/keyboard to Dear ImGui or my application?" about this.
  276: USING CUSTOM BACKEND / CUSTOM ENGINE
  277: ------------------------------------
  278: IMPLEMENTING YOUR PLATFORM BACKEND:
  279:  -> see https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/blob/master/docs/BACKENDS.md for basic instructions.
  280:  -> the Platform backends in impl_impl_XXX.cpp files contain many implementations.
  281: IMPLEMENTING YOUR RenderDrawData() function:
  282:  -> see https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/blob/master/docs/BACKENDS.md
  283:  -> the Renderer Backends in impl_impl_XXX.cpp files contain many implementations of a ImGui_ImplXXXX_RenderDrawData() function.
  284: IMPLEMENTING SUPPORT for ImGuiBackendFlags_RendererHasTextures:
  285:  -> see https://github.com/ocornut/imgui/blob/master/docs/BACKENDS.md
  286:  -> the Renderer Backends in impl_impl_XXX.cpp files contain many implementations of a ImGui_ImplXXXX_UpdateTexture() function.
  287:  Basic application/backend skeleton:
  288:      // Application init: create a Dear ImGui context, setup some options, load fonts
  289:      ImGui::CreateContext();
  290:      ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();
  291:      // TODO: set io.ConfigXXX values, e.g.
  292:      io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard;  // Enable keyboard controls
  293:      // TODO: Load TTF/OTF fonts if you don't want to use the default font.
  294:      io.Fonts->AddFontFromFileTTF("NotoSans.ttf");
  295:      // Application main loop
  296:      while (true)
  297:      {
  298:         // Setup low-level inputs, e.g. on Win32: calling GetKeyboardState(), or write to those fields from your Windows message handlers, etc.
  299:         // (In the examples/ app this is usually done within the ImGui_ImplXXX_NewFrame() function from one of the demo Platform Backends)
  300:         io.DeltaTime = 1.0f/60.0f;              // set the time elapsed since the previous frame (in seconds)
  301:         io.DisplaySize.x = 1920.0f;             // set the current display width
  302:         io.DisplaySize.y = 1280.0f;             // set the current display height here
  303:         io.AddMousePosEvent(mouse_x, mouse_y);  // update mouse position
  304:         io.AddMouseButtonEvent(0, mouse_b[0]);  // update mouse button states
  305:         io.AddMouseButtonEvent(1, mouse_b[1]);  // update mouse button states
  306:         // Call NewFrame(), after this point you can use ImGui::* functions anytime
  307:         // (So you want to try calling NewFrame() as early as you can in your main loop to be able to use Dear ImGui everywhere)
  308:         ImGui::NewFrame();
  309:         // Most of your application code here
  310:         ImGui::Text("Hello, world!");
  311:         MyGameUpdate(); // may use any Dear ImGui functions, e.g. ImGui::Begin("My window"); ImGui::Text("Hello, world!"); ImGui::End();
  312:         MyGameRender(); // may use any Dear ImGui functions as well!
  313:         // End the dear imgui frame
  314:         // (You want to try calling EndFrame/Render as late as you can, to be able to use Dear ImGui in your own game rendering code)
  315:         ImGui::EndFrame(); // this is automatically called by Render(), but available
  316:         ImGui::Render();
  317:         // Update textures
  318:         ImDrawData* draw_data = ImGui::GetDrawData();
  319:         for (ImTextureData* tex : *draw_data->Textures)
  320:             if (tex->Status != ImTextureStatus_OK)
  321:                 MyImGuiBackend_UpdateTexture(tex);
  322:         // Render dear imgui contents, swap buffers
  323:         MyImGuiBackend_RenderDrawData(draw_data);
  324:         SwapBuffers();
  325:      }
  326:      // Shutdown
  327:      ImGui::DestroyContext();
